WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:08.020
With nearly $4 billion in worldwide grosses
and counting, the Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:11.990
is clearly doing something right, but it's
still always tempting to imagine what might

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:15.829
have been if directors had chosen to do things
a little differently. Let's do a heroic double

00:00:15.829 --> 00:00:20.490
take with these Marvel movie alternate endings
you might not know about.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:21.400
BLADE (1998)

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.960
The version of Blade that made it to theaters
ends with our hero slaying a bunch of vampire

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.530
minions before settling in for a long-awaited
battle versus the movie's big bad guy, Deacon

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:34.199
Frost. Blade vanquishes Frost once and for
all with an overdose of anti-vampire poison,

00:00:34.199 --> 00:00:39.089
which he delivers by kicking a syringe right
into Frost's forehead...making him blow up.

00:00:39.089 --> 00:00:43.309
Kinda cheesy, but it works. Unlike the unused
alternate ending, which would have shown Frost

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:48.219
achieving his goal of becoming the Blood God
and morphing into a whirling fountain of blood...that

00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:52.460
still somehow has Frost's face in it. The
sequence would be pretty dumb even with today's

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.909
effects technology—so you can imagine how
bad it would've looked with the digital effects

00:00:55.909 --> 00:01:00.600
available during the '90s. Thankfully, we
were spared in the final cut.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:01.549
IRON MAN 2 (2010)

00:01:01.549 --> 00:01:05.750
The Iron Man sequel gave Tony Stark a pair
of bad guys to contend with: mad scientist

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:10.230
Ivan Vanko and Justin Hammer, a business rival
who partners with Vanko to steal the Iron

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:15.260
Man technology. Unfortunately for Hammer,
Vanko, AKA the villainous Whiplash, has a

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:19.250
few screws loose, and he isn't above double-crossing
his partner to achieve his ultimate goal of

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:23.570
revenge against Tony. Vanko ultimately ends
up dead and Hammer goes to jail, but in the

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:27.230
alternate ending, Vanko meets his maker a
little differently: blasted to bits at the

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.520
last minute after kidnapping Pepper Potts.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.800
THE WOLVERINE (2013)

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:36.310
The Wolverine from 2013 marked Hugh Jackman's
sixth outing as the iconic Marvel mutant,

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:39.920
but none of them gave him an opportunity to
wear his classic comic book costumes. Director

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.610
James Mangold looked to fix this problem in
his movie, though it still hit the cutting

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:47.620
room floor in the end. In the theatrical cut,
the film closes with Wolverine boarding a

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:51.560
jet and heading back to rejoin the X-Men.
But Mangold's unused ending also includes

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:54.950
a glimpse of his bodyguard Yukio presenting
him with a box containing his iconic blue-and-yellow

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:59.530
outfit from the comics. You should've worn
it in Days of Future Past, bub.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:01.490
ANT-MAN (2015)

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:05.950
Observant viewers may have noticed that Ant-Man
left a big loose end untied. After trying

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:10.479
to purchase Yellowjacket's size-altering technology,
HYDRA agent Mitchell Carson ultimately ends

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:14.000
up getting away with the tech during the movie's
final battle. Originally, director Peyton

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.120
Reed meant to finish his film by having Ant-Man
face off against Carson. But the crossover

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:22.210
demands of the Marvel Cinematic Universe dictated
a teaser prelude for Captain America: Civil

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:25.600
War, so that's a battle that will have to
wait for another day—perhaps during the

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.590
sequel.

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:28.720
IRON MAN 3 (2013)

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:32.130
In the original ending, Iron Man's foe the
Mandarin—actually an actor named Trevor

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:36.170
Slattery hired to distract our hero—is hauled
off to prison for his part in aiding Aldrich

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:40.130
Killian's evil schemes. That's a far kinder
fate than the one he suffers in an earlier

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:44.230
version of the script, in which Slattery uses
the unstable Extremis super-soldier serum.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:52.880
And like many of the other poor schmucks who
used it, he goes out with a bang.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:54.430
X-MEN ORIGINS: WOLVERINE (2009)

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:58.290
For many Marvel fans, one of the most disappointing
parts of X-Men Origins: Wolverine was what

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:02.800
the movie did with Deadpool. In Origins, the
"merc with a mouth" is reduced to a pale,

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.860
mute shadow of his comic book self. Director
Gavin Hood threw the character's fans a bone

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:10.709
with one of several post-credits scenes attached
to different prints of the movie. One sequence

00:03:10.709 --> 00:03:14.390
shows a hand emerging from rubble and reaching
out to touch Deadpool's severed head, after

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:18.380
which his eyes open and he "shushes" at the
camera. Maybe he was trying to tell the audience

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:21.500
not to ask for refunds.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.660
THE AMAZING SPIDER-MAN 2 (2014)

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:27.270
Comics readers know that Spider-Man has been
through a lot of changes over the years, some

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:30.950
more bizarre than others. He's always been
an orphan, with the death of his parents partially

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:35.250
driving him to play the hero after he was
bit by a radioactive spider. But in a deleted

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:39.580
ending to 2014's The Amazing Spider-Man 2,
Peter's father Richard reveals himself to

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:43.980
be very much alive. He even offers a variation
on Spider-Man's iconic motto, "with great

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:45.700
power comes great responsibility."

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:49.220
X-MEN: THE LAST STAND (2006)

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:53.560
Widely regarded as the worst entry in the
X-Men series, 2006's The Last Stand made a

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:57.310
number of unpopular decisions—like killing
off a bunch of beloved characters—that took

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:01.440
a soft franchise reboot to untangle. Even
though a series of alternate endings were

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.540
filmed for Last Stand, none of them would've
made the movie much better. So it's easy to

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:09.800
understand why glimpses of Beast returning
to help run Xavier's school, Rogue returning

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:13.610
to the academy with her powers returned, and
Wolverine going back to Canada, all ended

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:18.699
up on the cutting room floor. We still need
Professor X to mind-wipe our memories of the

00:04:18.699 --> 00:04:19.709
Juggernaut.

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:21.790
AVENGERS: AGE OF ULTRON (2015)

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:25.300
With the biggest cast in the Marvel Cinematic
Universe, the Avengers franchise has more

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:29.060
options than most when it comes to alternate
endings. Director Joss Whedon took full advantage

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:31.550
of this with 2015's Age of Ultron.

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:35.460
He briefly entertained the notion of working
Spider-Man into the story, an option nixed

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:39.350
by the studio, who still didn't have the rights
to the character during filmmaking. Whedon

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:42.970
also considered showing audiences a glimpse
of Captain Marvel in the final scene, another

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:46.680
idea Marvel Studios denied, since the character
hadn't yet been cast for her own standalone

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:50.840
feature. Finally, the director thought about
revealing that the slain Quicksilver was alive

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:55.160
after seemingly being gunned down during the
movie's climactic fight. However, Whedon correctly

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:59.080
realized that this would have cheapened the
character's death earlier in the movie. Still...we

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:01.680
wish we could've seen all the Avengers assemble.

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:05.160
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to see more videos like the one you

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:05.660
just saw.

